tandem (4.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The community list learns what produced a report. WineHQ's AppDB has had
    the rule for twenty years and this project had not adopted it: a test
    report is worthless unless you know exactly what produced it. Two shops can
    run the same program, need different verbs, and BOTH be right - one is on
    Wine 8 and the other on Wine 10 - and merging those two reports produces an
    answer that was never true anywhere. Each record now carries the Wine and
    winetricks versions, and the resolver weighs a report from a different
    major version at half.
  * A report also decays with age, and the decay is deliberately MILD - it has
    a floor of a quarter. That floor is the whole design: no amount of age lets
    ONE fresh report overturn a verb set four hundred machines confirmed. The
    "downgrade, do not overwrite" rule falls out of the arithmetic instead of
    being a rule of its own, so a sudden verb-set flip on an established
    fingerprint loses on weight until enough machines actually report it.
  * The three new fields are APPENDED and the header still says TANDEM-LISTA 1.
    Every reader of this format indexes by column, so appending is compatible
    by construction: a 4.6 client in the field reads fields 1-8 and ignores the
    rest, which is exactly its old behaviour. Bumping the version would make
    those clients reject the whole file and lose the list, to gain nothing. The
    rule to keep is append only - never reorder, never repurpose a column.
  * Each machine is counted once per lesson, without the list keeping anything
    that identifies a machine. The token is an HMAC of the file's identity
    under a secret that never leaves the sender, so the intake can tell that
    two records about THE SAME PROGRAM came from one machine - which is all
    deduplication needs - and cannot tell that two records about DIFFERENT
    programs did, because without the secret those tokens are unrelated values.
    It is not a machine identifier and cannot become one by accumulation. The
    rebuild does not republish it: it did its job at the intake, and putting it
    in a file the whole world downloads would turn a per-program token into
    something anybody can correlate against.
  * The leak sieve skips the three new fields, for a measured reason rather
    than for convenience: a Wine version with four numeric components
    ("9.0.0.1") is indistinguishable from an IPv4 address to its regex, so
    leaving them in its scope would park every honest record from such a
    machine for ever - and a parked record is a lesson that never leaves. The
    sieve guards FREE TEXT; a machine-generated field with a fixed shape gets a
    shape check instead, at the point it is built and again at the intake.

  * The published list can be signed, and a list changed after publication is
    refused. HTTPS proves the client talked to GitHub; it does not prove the
    bytes are what was published, and a bad row here is not a bad row on one
    machine - it is a verb name every Tandem downloads and may be asked to
    install. This is the third layer, under the verb-safety gate and the
    nothing-publishes-itself pull request.
  * The rollout order is the careful part: a signature that is PRESENT and
    WRONG rejects the file; a signature that is ABSENT does not, yet. Requiring
    it from day one would mean any hiccup in the signing step silently cuts
    every machine off from the list - and a quiet list is indistinguishable
    from a list with nothing to say.
  * This is the FIRST hand-made secret in the project, and CLAUDE.md said there
    were none for four versions. That sentence is corrected rather than quietly
    deleted, with the trade-off beside it: a key in Actions means whoever
    controls the repository can sign, so this does not protect against a bad
    row being merged - it protects against a copy modified anywhere that is not
    GitHub. The private half is generated by the owner on his own machine and
    has never touched this repository or an agent session; only the public half
    ships, and build.py packages without it rather than shipping a placeholder
    that would make every download fail verification.
  * A COM number Tandem gives is now a COM number Wine created. Measured
    against the installed Wine: mountmgr walks each family from index 0 and
    STOPS AT THE FIRST GAP, so with /dev/ttyUSB1 present and /dev/ttyUSB0
    absent it creates com1 for /dev/ttyS0 and nothing at all for the USB
    adapter. Tandem listed every device that existed and numbered them in
    sequence, so the owner was told his pinpad was on COM2 when Wine had
    created no COM2 - wrong in the invisible direction, inside the one command
    written to end exactly this confusion. A hole is not exotic: unplug and
    replug an adapter, or use a two-port converter, and you have one. The
    devices Wine skipped are now named, with the command that rescues them.
  * The USB printer message has never fired on any machine on Earth. It looked
    for /dev/usblp0; the kernel's usblp driver registers its class device
    through a usblp_devnode() that PREPENDS "usb/", so the node has always been
    /dev/usb/lp0. The repository already contradicted itself about this -
    alternativas.tsv says /dev/usb/lp0 correctly, in all seven languages -
    which means a complete translated sentence naming the exact fix command sat
    behind a glob that could never match. For shop software printing IS the
    program, and there was no printing code anywhere else in the tree.
  * A vendor daemon installed from a script is recognised as running. Thales
    names the Sentinel daemons aksusbd_x86_64 and hasplmd_x86_64, and the check
    was an exact pgrep - so on a machine where the runtime came from the
    vendor's script rather than from a .deb, Tandem said "not running" about a
    daemon that is running, and sent the owner to fix something that is not
    broken. The suffix is matched explicitly rather than by loosening to a
    substring, because loose matching is how "wine" starts matching
    "winetricks".
 -- Tandem <rockx0@gmail.com>  Fri, 14 Aug 2026 04:03:22 +0000

tandem (4.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Column 4 of limites.tsv is translated. It is the WAY OUT - what a
    technician can actually do about a protection dongle - and it was
    byte-identical Portuguese in all seven tables, the English default
    included: 15 rows, one md5 across limites.tsv, .es, .fr, .zh_CN, .hi and
    .ar. So the product told a French or Chinese shop owner that his case had
    a way out and then handed him the instructions in a language he cannot
    read, which is worse than saying nothing.
  * The check that was supposed to catch this greps for a phrase that lives in
    column 3, so for two versions it only ever proved column 3 had moved. The
    suite now checksums column 4 against the Portuguese source and fails if any
    table is byte-identical to it - asserting that somebody translated the
    column, not what they wrote in it, because the wording is a translator's
    business and has to stay free to change.
  * The first version of the tool that did the translation walked the tables by
    LINE NUMBER. The per-language tables carry their own comment headers and are
    not the same length, so it mismatched the rows - it would have handed a
    CodeMeter owner the Sentinel instructions. It joins on the signature in
    column 1 now, which the suite already asserts is identical and in the same
    order across all seven tables.

  * A saved web page is named as one by every handler, not only by .deb. A
    download that goes wrong rarely produces nothing: the site answers with a
    404, a login wall or a robot check, and the browser saves that HTML under
    the name the link promised. t_parece_pagina_web has existed since 3.8 with
    exactly two callers, so everywhere else the owner was told his download
    stopped part way - which sends him back to the same link to fetch the same
    page, for ever. The ordering is the interesting half and both cases were
    measured: an HTML page named .jar fails as "zip_invalido", because a zip's
    index lives at the END of the file, so it is indistinguishable from a real
    truncation; and the .rpm reader answers "nao_e_rpm" rather than a
    truncation token, so a check wired only into the incomplete-download branch
    never fires.
  * "Why this component?" - the screen where the owner agrees to a half-hour
    download now names the file the program actually asked for, not just the
    runtime: "Visual C++ 2015-2022 - the program asked for msvcp140.dll,
    vcruntime140.dll". Tandem has known that pair since 3.3, where it exists so
    delivery can be proved afterwards, and was throwing the useful half away.
    It explains more and executes exactly the same.
  * The .exe is asked whether the download finished. The same question is
    already put to a .jar, an .AppImage and a .deb; the .exe was the one reader
    never asked, and a 400 MB point-of-sale installer cut short over a shop
    connection is the commonest broken thing that reaches this project. Wine's
    answer was "Bad EXE format", after the wait. The check is ONE-SIDED on
    purpose: only "shorter than its own headers declare" is a verdict, because
    NSIS and Inno Setup append their payload after the last section, so every
    real installer is legitimately longer than its section table accounts for.
  * The explanation for a failed dependency install came from the wrong verb.
    MARCA_WT was reassigned on every iteration of the install loop, successes
    included, so after the loop it marked the last verb ATTEMPTED rather than
    the last one that FAILED - and the whole cause table then ran over that
    slice. When a program needs two components and the first fails, the owner
    was told his internet had failed, about a component whose real problem was
    something else entirely, because a LATER component downloaded normally. He
    goes and looks at his router. Every test in the suite installs a single
    verb, which is why nothing could see it.
  * That table is now t_causa_do_winetricks in common.sh instead of twenty
    lines of elif inline in the loop, so it can be exercised without making a
    real winetricks fail - which is the reason the defect above survived. The
    two-verb case is a fixture, and the suite asserts BOTH directions: the
    failing verb's own slice names the real cause, and the old whole-tail slice
    is shown still blaming the internet.
  * The date in the wrong-clock message is no longer written the way only
    Brazil writes it. Three sites, all of them feeding a sentence that IS
    translated: the winetricks cause table, the apt cause table, and the
    timestamp on the tandem socorro report - the file the owner sends to
    whoever is helping him, who may be anywhere.
  * The strongest evidence this project ever gets was being thrown away. A
    verb reaches the repeated-receipt branch when Wine's OWN LOADER has just
    asked for its DLL again while the prefix carries a permanent receipt
    saying that verb was installed - the loader contradicting the receipt, in
    writing, in the same log. traducao-suspeita.tsv is the work list that has
    already found six wrong entries in the table, and t_anota_suspeita was
    called from two sites, both at install time, so that moment reached
    nothing at all. The receipt is KEPT: rule 4 is about not paying twice, and
    the verb may well have delivered exactly what it promised while the
    program wants something else. This records and reports; it does not retry
    and does not undo.
  * And the owner is told which file is still being asked for, instead of only
    "I already installed what this program was asking for" - a sentence Wine's
    log contradicts on the line above it.
  * The dedup on that list is OPT-IN, and the default stays append. From the
    install path a repeat means "installed again, another day, and again
    failed to deliver", which is a count worth having and is why the date
    column exists; an existing test was pinning that and the test was right.
    Only the new caller asks for the dedup, because it fires every time the
    owner double-clicks a program whose receipt the loader contradicts - a
    shop opening the same broken program every morning would otherwise append
    the same pair every morning, for ever.
  * Wine saying it has not implemented something is a verdict now. It is the
    third failure class the loop had no branch for - the dependency exists,
    Wine has not finished implementing part of it, and there is NOTHING to
    install - and the answer was "the program closed with error (code 53)",
    which sends the owner looking for a defect in a machine that is fine.
    Tandem names the function and the Wine version and stops there: it does
    not manage Wine, and telling a shopkeeper to upgrade one by hand is not an
    instruction he can follow safely.
  * The two Wine wordings were taken from the INSTALLED Wine's own format
    strings rather than from memory, and only one of them is a verdict.
    "No implementation for X imported from Y, setting to Z" is Wine stubbing
    an export at LOAD time and carrying on - programs import functions they
    never call all the time, so that line is in the log of software that works
    perfectly, and reporting it would be a false alarm on a working program.
    "Call from ... to unimplemented function X, aborting" is the program
    having actually called it and Wine having given up. Only the second is
    matched, and the suite asserts both directions.
  * Two programs no longer install into one prefix at the same time. The lock
    tandem-exe takes at the top is keyed to the FILE, deliberately, so that
    opening two different programs keeps working - but both of those programs
    land in the same prefix by default and both may decide they need
    components, so two "winetricks -q" runs could be writing into one
    WINEPREFIX at once. That is exactly what the file lock's own comment says
    corrupts a prefix, and the lock chosen to prevent it did not cover the
    case: the prefix lock existed and wrapped only wineboot, which is the one
    moment two processes were never going to collide on anyway.
  * The waiter says why it is waiting. dotnet48 takes half an hour, and half
    an hour of silence is the failure this project is named after. A lock that
    cannot be CREATED still lets the program open, which is the same decision,
    for the same reason, as the file lock above it: an impossible lock and a
    busy lock are different cases.
