tandem (4.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The community list answered with the FIRST row it happened to read, so
    whoever got into the file earliest owned that program for ever. Measured on
    a two-row list: 3 machines confirming vcrun2010 beat 400 confirming
    vcrun2022, and the 400 were dead text nobody would ever read.
  * It also never added two rows up. Merging reports is the entire job of the
    machine count, and two rows saying "vcrun2022, 200 machines" answered 200 -
    which is not only a wrong number, it changes which lesson wins.
  * And the count shown to the owner could describe a different lesson than the
    one being offered: t_lista_maquinas did not filter by confidence at all, so
    a rejected row sitting above a confirmed one supplied the number. Measured:
    verbs from a 40-machine confirmation, presented as "7 machines".
  * A verb set most machines report as NOT working is no longer suggested. 2
    confirmations against 300 rejections used to win, because rejections were
    not read at all - and spreading an error takes no more effort than
    spreading a fix.
  * Ties break on the most recent report, then on the fewest verbs, then
    alphabetically, so the answer cannot depend on the order of the rows.
  * A redirect was being counted as a delivery. curl with no -L does not follow
    a 301 and exits 0, and the queue is rewritten from what did NOT go - so the
    lesson was deleted from the only place it existed. The status code is read
    explicitly now and only 2xx counts. The redirect is still not followed, on
    purpose: its target is chosen by whatever answered, not by anybody here.
  * wget lost the same line by a different route - it follows redirects by
    default and turns the POST into a GET on the way, exiting 0 having posted
    nothing. --max-redirect=0.
  * The daily ceiling only ever counted the lines that WENT, so a failure cost
    nothing: a machine with no route to the address retried every queued line
    on every run, for ever, at twenty seconds of timeout each. A hundred
    queued lessons is over half an hour of a background process that was never
    going to succeed, started again every time somebody opens a program. Three
    refusals in a row now end the pass, and the queue waits an hour.
  * Two passes over the queue could run at once - sending is spawned detached
    every time a program is confirmed, and "tandem enviar agora" starts one
    too. Both truncated the same ".resto" file and both then moved it over the
    queue, so the truncation landed in the middle of the other pass's appends
    and the lines already written were gone. One lock, on the same pattern as
    tandem-exe: an impossible lock is not a busy lock, and carries on without.
  * The buttons of "did this program work as you expected?" were Portuguese in
    a shipped release, and so were five other Install/Cancel/Not now pairs. The
    literal counter reads the FIRST argument of a message call and stops, so a
    line whose text is a clean t_msg lookup passed as finished while the two
    words the owner actually clicks were never looked at by anything. Three of
    the six buttons already had catalogue keys nobody had wired up.
  * Worse, and larger: the prose-body rule keys off function NAMES - t_texto_*,
    t_causa_*, acao_*, uso - and the eleven handler executables define no
    functions at all. They are straight-line scripts. So in tandem-repair,
    tandem-deb, tandem-jar and eight others, that rule and the printf rule were
    dead code, and what they missed was the whole report of "tandem repair" -
    the command an owner is told to run when a double click opens the wrong
    program. It printed "Associações reaplicadas", "antes:", "agora:" and a
    sentence telling them to log out and back in, in Portuguese, on a tree
    whose counter said TOTAL 0. That report is now one message per row,
    with the type and both handlers as data, so a translator places the labels
    instead of receiving them as fragments.
  * Widening the scope to whole files needs an exclusion rule, and it is about
    where an argument GOES rather than what it looks like - "it looks like code"
    is the guess that has failed thirteen times. A value handed to
    t_memoria_grava lands in a state file and stays Portuguese by a standing
    decision; an argument to t_como_root is executed, not read; a grep or sed
    argument is a program for another tool.
  * And the fifteenth is a whole FILE TYPE nothing had ever looked at. ALVOS
    globs *.sh and src/bin, so in fifteen revisions the counter had never opened
    a .py - and the six Python readers RAISED PORTUGUESE. About thirty
    sentences: "nao comeca com ELF", "o pacote nao traz um arquivo control",
    "zip invalido ou incompleto". Every one of them reached the owner verbatim,
    because the handlers print the ERRO field through "nao consegui ler".
  * The catch-all branch was worse than untranslated. print("ERRO=%s" % e) hands
    over a raw Python exception - "[Errno 13] Permission denied" - which is
    English jargon whatever language the owner reads. That path now says what
    happened in words and puts the exception in the log, where the person who
    is helping will read it.
  * So the ERRO field carries a TOKEN, never a sentence, and t_erro_do_leitor
    turns a token into a sentence in the owner's language - 25 tokens, 26 new
    messages, seven languages. An unknown token gets a generic sentence rather
    than the key name, because t_msg prints the key when a key is missing, which
    is right for a log and would be jargon on a shop counter. A token with a
    character that is not a name is refused: the field comes out of a program,
    and a program's output is input.
  * Counting that would not have held it, so the suite reads the READERS: every
    token they can emit must have a catalogue key, and no reader may go back to
    raising a sentence. A reader that grows a failure without a message fails in
    the commit that adds it. The first version of that check found 21 of 25
    tokens and reported "missing: none" - a completeness check that was itself
    incomplete on its first run, in exactly the way the counter has been fifteen
    times. It knew about raise but not about print.
  * Half the suite's expected values were not actually guarded. The checksum
    that exists so a bulk rename cannot quietly rewrite a comparison is
    line-based, and 206 of 415 "equal" calls are written across two lines -
    which is what you do as soon as an expected value is long, so precisely the
    values most at risk were the ones nothing watched. It reads the file with
    continuations joined now: 419 of 415 call sites and 93 of 97 case patterns,
    the other four being bare fallbacks with no data in them.
  * Joining the lines then made that guard checksum ITS OWN value, because
    "grep -oE" cuts each match off after the second argument, so the filter
    that drops the guard's own two lines never saw what identified them. Two
    numbers chasing each other on every edit is a guard that can never be
    green. The filter runs before the extraction now.

