tandem (4.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The literal counter can finally see the zenity panel, which is the only
    screen a shop owner who never opens a terminal ever sees. It could not:
    acao_painel builds its eighteen menu rows as bare arguments to zenity,
    inside a command substitution that is itself the whole value of a string,
    and every version of the counter either skipped substitutions outright or
    chopped them into shell debris with a regex.
  * That is the ELEVENTH and TWELFTH time this measure has been narrower than
    reality, and every one of the twelve erred the same way - reporting that
    work was finished when it was not. The number has now gone 0 -> 75 -> 107
    -> 167 -> 145, where only the last step is a reduction: it is 22 named
    exceptions, the command names the panel matches on and the hardware labels
    that must not move with the language.
  * The rule stops chasing syntax. Inside a body that exists to produce prose,
    every double-quoted string is examined, whatever surrounds it - assignment,
    append, printf argument, zenity argument. A rule about syntax is what
    failed twelve times; a rule about content with an auditable list of
    exceptions cannot quietly grow to cover new prose.
  * Finding those strings needs a walk rather than a regex, and the walk has to
    make one distinction: outside a string a $( ) is a command whose arguments
    may be messages, so descend into it; inside a string it is data being
    interpolated, so its text is not part of that sentence even though the
    command inside it may carry messages of its own.
  * The privacy sieve was refusing whole machines and DELETING what it refused.
    t_lista_vaza matched the owner's user name as a substring of the entire
    record, and the record's first field is a 32-character hex fingerprint - so
    a name that happens to be hex matched it. Measured: an owner called "a",
    "e" or "f" had 5 of 5 clean records refused, "ed" 3 of 5. Those are
    ordinary Unix names.
  * Worse than refusing: a refused line was destroyed. t_envio_envia skipped it
    without writing it to the file that then REPLACES the queue, so the sieve
    was quietly erasing the lessons it exists to protect. A refusal now HOLDS
    the line. Stopping a record from leaving the machine is a different power
    from being allowed to delete it.
  * It also let through three of the things docs/LIST-FORMAT.md promises a
    record cannot carry: a bare file name ("PDVSuperMax-4.2-setup.exe" names a
    shop's software in the clear), a Windows path, an e-mail address and a MAC.
    Fields are compared one at a time now, with one rule per documented bullet.
  * A short name is matched as a whole word rather than anywhere in a field,
    because one or two hex characters occur in almost every fingerprint - and
    the machine this was measured on is called "vm". Dropping short names would
    have been simpler and would have quietly abandoned the promise.
  * Now that a refusal only holds a line back, erring toward refusal is cheap
    and erring toward sending is not: "works on 1.2.3.4" is refused even though
    it is plainly a version number, because telling that from an address in
    free text is not reliably possible and the two mistakes do not cost the
    same.
  * None of this was reachable in a shipped version, because TANDEM_LISTA_ENVIO
    is empty and t_envio_envia returns before the sieve runs. It would all have
    fired on the day an address was filled in.
  * The test fixture is what hid it: the identity in the sieve's tests was
    "abc123", and a six-character stand-in cannot collide with a user name the
    way a real 32-hex fingerprint does.

  * The 4.3 entry had to be rewrapped, and there is a guard for it now: lintian
    refuses a changelog line over 80 columns, the release step runs it with
    --fail-on warning and demands zero output, and eight lines at 81 and 82
    columns turned a green suite into a red release. Found by CI rather than by
    the suite, which is the wrong order to find it in.
  * Three defects on the paths that DO work, found by investigating a receiver
    for the community list and then not building one. The prefilled issue form
    named a field that does not exist: the client sent "linha" while list.yml
    calls the field "record", so the one shortcut that works opened an EMPTY
    form while both READMEs promise "the form already filled in". A parameter
    matching nothing looks exactly like one matching something, so the test
    compares the two files rather than trusting either.
  * The same offer could fail in silence. With no browser xdg-open exits
    non-zero and says why, but it was backgrounded with its output discarded,
    so clicking "open the form" did nothing at all. It runs in the foreground
    now and a failure prints the address, in every language, so the owner can
    open it on a phone.
  * And a recipe could write arbitrary fields into a community-list record.
    Only its verbs were validated; ARQUITETURA, LIMITE, RESULTADO and PROGRAMA
    were copied verbatim, ARQUITETURA goes into field 2 of the record, and a
    TAB survives - so a recipe, the file whose own header tells the owner to
    accept it from other people, could splice the remaining fields including
    the note. A shop's name onto a public list, and a tagging primitive
    besides: a unique string per victim, readable back off the list.
  * The receiver itself is NOT built, and that is the recommendation rather
    than a delay. The list is not empty for want of an address: only tandem-exe
    writes the keys a record needs, so eight of nine formats can never produce
    one; the down-path has a single caller; and no known public program needs a
    verb. An anonymous intake would also have to either fabricate the machine
    count, which is the literal 1, or keep source addresses - a database
    joining Brazilian shops to the commercial software each one runs.
  * The zenity panel is translated. It is the only screen a shop owner who
    never opens a terminal ever sees, and it was the last thing in the program
    still hard-coded in Portuguese: eighteen menu rows, the window's own
    question, both column headings, the file chooser's title and its two filter
    labels.
  * The action column stays Portuguese, and that is the standing decision
    rather than an omission: "case $esc in" matches those names, and a command
    copied off a forum has to work on any machine. So the panel is now split
    down the middle - the half a person reads comes from the catalogue in seven
    languages, and the half the shell matches never moves.
  * The file chooser's globs moved out of the message and into a variable at
    the top of the file. They are the nine formats, not prose, and a translator
    opening a catalogue should not be shown a list of extensions to consider
    rewording.
  * Verified by resolving the rows in all seven languages rather than by
    reading the code: a key present in the code and missing from a catalogue
    falls back silently, so the test demands a real sentence per language and
    fails on the key name.
  * tandem doctor is translated - the second most-read screen in the program,
    and the one "tandem socorro" ships to whoever is helping. 55 keys: every
    section heading, every label, and every yes/NO line with the explanation
    that follows it. An English-speaking user ran the flagship diagnostic and
    got Portuguese, and that had been true of every published version.
  * The indentation stayed in the code rather than going into the catalogue. A
    translator should see a sentence, not two leading spaces they have to
    preserve exactly, and a report whose columns depend on invisible characters
    in a .po file is a report that will drift.
  * The report's own first line - the product name and its version - stays a
    literal, with the reason written into the counter's exception list rather
    than left to be rediscovered: a translated product name is a name that
    finds nothing.
  * Verified on the installed package, not in the tree: built the .deb,
    installed it, and read the report back in all seven languages. The first
    attempt did not prove anything - apt declined to replace a package of the
    same version and the old catalogue answered - and the attempt after that
    was refused by build.py because the catalogues had drifted from po/, which
    is that guard doing exactly its job.
  * The autoteste report is translated - all 35 of its lines, pass and fail
    together with the explanation each failure carries. It is the command that
    answers "does this actually work on my machine", and its answer was in one
    language.
  * The remaining window titles and buttons went with it: the program picker,
    the uninstall picker, and Remove / Cancel / Remove anyway / Stop. A dialog
    whose buttons are in a language the reader does not have is a dialog they
    close rather than answer.
  * The literal counter now skips t_diz lines, because the log is a different
    audience with a documented exception - it is read by whoever is helping,
    not by the shop owner. That is a rule about syntax, which is what failed
    twelve times, and it is defensible only because t_diz cannot reach the user
    at all: it appends to a file. Two tests pin it, including one where a
    message shares a line with a log call.
  * The migration is finished: the counter reads 0 and the last thirteen
    sentences are gone. The hardware-key advice, the virtual-machine paragraph
    which is the longest message in the program, the data screen, both list
    screens and the contribute screen. 584 keys in each of seven catalogues.
  * A key-name collision produced a WRONG MESSAGE, silently, and it is the one
    thing in this version worth reading twice. lista_nao_baixou already existed
    meaning "I could not download the list"; reusing that name for the "you
    have not downloaded it yet" screen meant the new text was dropped as a
    duplicate and the screen showed a download failure instead. Nothing
    errored. Found by running the command in three languages and reading the
    output, which is the only method that has ever caught this class of thing.
  * The counter says 0 again, and the last time it said 0 the 0 was false. What
    is different is not the number but the instrument: it now catches a bare
    zenity argument inside a command substitution inside a string, an append
    into any variable name, a printf whose prose is in the argument rather than
    the format, and a heredoc - the four shapes behind the previous false
    zeros. The comment above the ratchet says so, so nobody trusts the number
    without reading why.
  * The hardware-key tests now expect ENGLISH, and that is not a slip: the
    prose moved into po/, English is the default, and the suite runs without a
    forced locale. What they assert is unchanged - that the sentence says "I
    could not check" rather than condemning the machine.

 -- ChrnX0 <rockx0@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0000

tandem (4.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * SECURITY. A verb name coming from outside this machine could run arbitrary
    code as the user, and the way in was the feature built for sharing: a
    recipe file, whose own header says "you can read it, check it and send it to
    somebody else".
  * Two holes, both in the shape of the name rather than its characters. A
    leading dash was allowed, so "--self-update" and "-q" were passed to
    winetricks as its own options - the first makes winetricks overwrite itself
    from the internet. And a dot was allowed, while the shipped winetricks
    SOURCES an argument matching *.verb as a shell script, from the current
    directory - and Tandem changes into the double-clicked file's folder before
    it ever calls winetricks. A zip carrying setup.exe, evil.verb and a recipe
    naming that verb was arbitrary code. Zero of winetricks' own 538 verb names
    contain a dot, so refusing it costs nothing.
  * A lesson from somebody else's machine may now install a DEPENDENCY and
    nothing else. winetricks labels 112 of its verbs "settings", and that is
    where the damage lives: "sandbox" and "isolate_home" remove the
    environment's links to your home folder, "remove_mono" takes .NET support
    out, "winxp" moves the Windows version under an installed program's feet.
    Every one of them passed every check, installed cleanly and earned a
    permanent receipt. The authority is winetricks itself, so this keeps working
    as winetricks changes, with a named fallback when it cannot be read.
  * Both doors use the stricter check - the community list and recipe import -
    and the check happens where the verb is USED, not only where it arrives, so
    a verb that a recipe wrote into the local memory on some earlier day is
    caught too.
  * Verified by running the attack: four hostile recipes refused, including one
    hiding "sandbox" behind a legitimate verb, a legitimate recipe still
    imported, and the payload never ran. The previous validator accepted all
    four.

  * Sending what Tandem learns to the community list is ON BY DEFAULT. It was
    born off, and the measurable consequence was that the list stayed empty: a
    default nobody changes is a decision made by the default.
  * The owner is TOLD rather than asked, twice. dpkg prints the notice at
    install, in the language the machine is set to, read out of the installed
    catalogue with awk and never sourced - postinst runs as root, and a file
    that will one day arrive from a translator must not execute there. The same
    notice appears again the first time there is a real line to send, with that
    line displayed in full rather than described.
  * Turning it off is one command, tandem enviar nao, it is named in both
    notices, and it sticks - a decision that reset on upgrade would not be one.
  * What makes a default defensible rather than merely convenient is not the
    default: the record cannot carry a filename, a path, a user name, a machine
    name or an IP, and the sieve that guarantees that runs twice. There are now
    tests for the sieve itself, including one that a CLEAN record passes - a
    sieve that refuses everything would have gone green without it.

  * The messages are gettext .po files now. po/ is the source of truth and
    src/lib/idiomas/*.txt is generated from it; build.py refuses to package a
    catalogue that has drifted from its .po.
  * The reason is a defect the hand-rolled format could not even detect: when an
    English message changes, the six translations keep the old text and nothing
    says so. msgmerge marks that entry fuzzy, the compiler drops a fuzzy entry,
    and the reader gets English instead of a sentence describing behaviour
    Tandem no longer has.
  * It also buys plural forms - Arabic has six, Chinese has one, and the old
    format could only write "1 linha(s)" - and it buys Poedit and Weblate, which
    is what the five unreviewed catalogues actually need.
  * gettext is not used at build or run time: msgfmt would break the rule that
    the packager depends on nothing, and %1$s would undo the {1} substitution
    that exists because paths carry percent signs.

  * English is the default. An unrecognised locale gets English, and English is
    what every catalogue falls back to for a missing key. It used to be
    Portuguese, which protected nobody: a Portuguese machine resolves to
    Portuguese by locale anyway, so the only people that default ever reached
    were those whose language Tandem has no catalogue for.
  * The help screen is translated. uso() was a heredoc - forty lines of the
    most-read text in the program - and six versions of the literal counter had
    scored it as zero, because a heredoc is neither a call nor an assignment nor
    a printf.
  * The manual now follows the same rule: English at
    /usr/share/man/man1/tandem.1, Portuguese at the per-locale path
    /usr/share/man/pt_BR/man1/tandem.1 that man(1) itself looks in.
  * The .desktop files carry all seven languages, with English in the bare
    Name=/Comment= and the rest in Name[xx]=.
  * alternativas.tsv and limites.tsv are English now, with the Portuguese rows
    in *.pt_BR.tsv, so the unsuffixed file is the default in every format that
    has one.

  * The translation is complete. tools/conta-literais.py reports TOTAL 0 across
    src/bin and src/lib, and the suite asserts that number, so the next literal
    added anywhere fails a test. 404 keys in each of the seven catalogues.
  * The data tables translate too. alternativas.tsv and limites.tsv carry prose
    in their columns and it reaches the owner, so they now work like the
    catalogues: a per-language table beside the original, with the original as
    the fallback. All six exist - 43 alternative rows and 37 impossibility rows
    in en, es, fr, zh_CN, hi and ar.
  * What is asserted about a translated table matters more than its wording:
    the same data rows, the same pattern column in the same order, and the same
    class column. A reordered row would match the wrong program; a translated
    class would pick the wrong message to frame it with.

  * Three defects found by investigating a feature that was then NOT built, and
    all three measured against real Wine rather than read out of the source.
  * Wine's own copy of a DLL no longer counts as a delivery. Wine installs some
    560 DLLs of its own into every prefix, marked "Wine builtin DLL" at offset
    64 of the file - 560 of 560 measured on a virgin prefix. verbos.tsv maps
    dotnet48 to mscoree.dll, and Wine had already put an mscoree.dll in both
    system32 and syswow64, so the proof of delivery for the most expensive verb
    in the project could not fail: an install that did nothing was approved, and
    under rule number 4 the receipt is permanent. Half an hour of the owner's
    time, and "I already installed what this program was asking for" ever after.
  * The identity freeze had never once run. Measured on Wine 9.0: wineboot
    writes a RANDOM MachineGuid into the 64-bit view while creating the prefix,
    so the old guard - "the registry value is absent" - was never true, while
    the mark file recorded a GUID the prefix did not contain. Remake the
    environment and a program that ties its licence to this machine sees a
    different machine, which is the exact loss that code exists to prevent. It
    now keys on the mark file, and it writes BOTH registry views: "wine reg"
    here runs 32-bit, so a write with no /reg: flag landed in Wow6432Node while
    the read looked at the 64-bit key - the code wrote the view it did not read.
  * "tandem identidade" shows the two views and says so when they disagree. That
    is the check that would have caught the line above, and it was missing.
  * A 64-bit program in a 32-bit environment is now recognised BEFORE running,
    off the #arch= line Wine writes into system.reg. Nothing in the tree had
    ever read that line; the case was only ever diagnosed afterwards, by
    grepping English out of Wine's log. Only a DECLARED win32 refuses - the
    folder layout answers the same question, but a deduction is not a fact and
    refusing to open a working program on a guess would be the worse defect.
  * t_identidade_fixa refuses a prefix without our mark. Rule number 1 was
    documented in that function and enforced only by its caller.
  * A registry view that refuses the write no longer freezes the prefix that
    way. The first version of the fix above wrote its mark whatever happened
    AND made the mark the guard, so a prefix where one view failed kept 32-bit
    and 64-bit programs on different machine identifiers forever, with nothing
    to retry it - the same lie one shape further out. The recorded value is the
    guard now: empty means try again. A win32 prefix has one view and is
    stamped in it, because demanding both there would be a refusal dressed up
    as a safety check.

  * "tandem doctor" and the graphical panel are still in Portuguese, and this
    release says so instead of claiming otherwise. The literal counter reported
    TOTAL 0 and the test suite asserted that number; both were wrong. The
    counter's assignment pattern was a whitelist of variable names, and
    acao_doctor assembles its whole report by appending into one that was not on
    the list - so the diagnostic an English-speaking user reads, and the text
    "tandem socorro" sends to whoever is helping them, were counted as zero.
    Fixing that exposed a tenth blind spot: printf's prose does not have to be
    in the format string, which hid the longest message in the program.
  * It was found by installing the built package and reading the output. That is
    the only method that has ever caught this measure lying, and it has now lied
    ten times.
  * The true number is 75 and the suite asserts it as a ratchet - it may fall,
    never rise. A hard zero that is wrong is worse than a true 75 that can only
    shrink: the zero says the work is finished and the 75 says where it is not.
    Those 75 sentences are the next release's work.

 -- ChrnX0 <rockx0@gmail.com>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000

tandem (4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * "tandem portas" no longer offers a shopkeeper 32 sockets this machine does
    not have. Found on a photograph of a real counter: the list ran COM1 to
    COM32 and put the shop's one real device, a USB adapter, on COM33. The
    source said "a PC already has three or four /dev/ttyS*". It has
    THIRTY-TWO - the kernel's 8250 driver registers 32 lines whether or not
    any serial hardware exists, and a modern PC has none of them.
  * serial_core publishes the UART type at /sys/class/tty/ttySN/type, mode
    444, so no privilege is needed: 0 is PORT_UNKNOWN, meaning the kernel
    probed that line and found nothing. A run of those now collapses into one
    line that says what they are, and the device the shop actually owns is
    marked so the eye lands on it. The report went from 35 lines to 12.
  * The numbering still counts the phantoms, because Wine counts them. The
    tempting fix - renumber the device to COM1 - would print a port the
    program is never told about.
  * Being unable to inspect a port means treating it as real. Hiding one that
    exists would break the only thing this section is for, and that is worse
    than printing a line too many.

  * A file that is not there is no longer reported as a file whose type is
    unknown. Found on a photograph of a real screen: the owner got "I did not
    recognise the type of this file" and went hunting for a defect in Tandem's
    format support. There was no file. acao_install routed by extension and,
    when the name said nothing, sniffed the contents with "head ...
    2>/dev/null" - so a path that does not exist failed every test in silence
    and landed on the last else. Naming the WRONG cause is worse than naming
    none.
  * A mistyped command now says so. The case at the bottom of the script sends
    every unrecognised word to acao_install, so "tandem intalar" was answered
    with a complaint about file types. A word with no slash, no dot and no
    match on disk is almost never a file.
  * A folder, and a file with no read permission, each get their own sentence.
  * An error page saved under a program's name is called what it is. A
    download that goes wrong rarely produces nothing: the site answers with a
    404 or a login wall and the browser saves that HTML as programa.deb. Every
    reader here would otherwise report its own local disappointment - "no ar
    signature" - which is true, useless, jargon, and points at the wrong
    place.
  * The unknown-type message now names the file and lists what Tandem can
    actually open, so it is a sentence somebody can act on.

  * Tandem speaks seven languages. Until here every sentence was a Portuguese
    literal sitting inside the script that printed it, which was right while
    the product had one owner in one country and stopped being right the
    moment somebody elsewhere installed it.
  * The messages are DATA, not code, and the format is built so they cannot be
    anything else. A catalogue is parsed by "read" and never evaluated, so a
    dollar sign, a backtick or a $(...) inside a translation is text and stays
    text. Translation files will one day arrive from strangers, and a
    translator is not somebody who should have to know what a subshell is.
  * Substitution is {1} {2} and deliberately not printf's %s. Paths and
    versions carry percent signs, and this project has been bitten by a format
    string before; there is a test with a folder called "50% off" in it.
  * A key missing from a translation falls back to the Portuguese original -
    never to the key name, never to nothing. A half-finished translation still
    says something a person can read. There is also a test that every key
    exists in every catalogue, so half-finished does not become permanent by
    accident.
  * Which language: TANDEM_IDIOMA in the environment, then the owner's choice,
    then the system's own locale, then Portuguese. pt_PT gets the Portuguese
    catalogue and es_AR the Spanish one, because the country is not the
    language. Anything unrecognised stays on Portuguese rather than changing
    language under the people already using it.
  * Reading the system's language had to be moved to the very top of the
    library. The charmap fixup below it exports LC_ALL so zenity can print an
    accent, and that overwrote the only evidence of what language the machine
    was in: on any computer with no pt_BR locale generated - which is most of
    them outside Brazil - every user looked like they had asked for C.
  * The question is asked on FIRST RUN, not during the package install. dpkg
    holds a lock while postinst runs, the graphical installer runs it in a
    daemon with no terminal, and the per-user work has no reliable way to know
    who the user is. Following the language the system is already set to costs
    nothing and is right far more often than a dialog nobody sees.
  * A language whose script has no font installed is refused with a sentence,
    not accepted into a screen of empty boxes - the zenity-accents lesson, one
    alphabet further out. Being unable to check means going ahead: refusing on
    a machine we could not inspect would be worse.
  * The five translations nobody has reviewed yet say so, on the list and
    again when you pick one. Shipping a translation no speaker has read is
    defensible; shipping it without saying so is not.
  * Languages: Portuguese (the original), English, Spanish, French, Simplified
    Chinese, Hindi and Arabic.

 -- ChrnX0 <rockx0@gmail.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000

tandem (4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Two of the four rows in "what will never work" were checked against
    primary sources and did not survive. This release is the correction, and
    the correction is bigger than the wording: it changes what a shop owner is
    told when the answer is not "no".
  * Hardware keys are no longer one verdict. Thales publishes "Supporting
    Protected Applications Under Wine" saying both SL and HL Sentinel keys
    have been tested, naming Wine 10.0 - the exact Wine on the reference
    machine - once the Linux Run-time Environment is installed. The key is
    never touched from the Windows side: a Linux daemon owns it and the
    program reaches it over the machine's own network. Tandem was telling
    those owners their case was impossible. HASP4 and Hardlock, which the
    manufacturer itself excludes, keep the old verdict and are now told apart
    by name.
  * limites.tsv gained a fourth column: the way out. A row without one is a
    dead end and is still said plainly. A row with one produces a different
    message - "this has a way", followed by the way. CodeMeter, Rockey, PC/SC
    card readers and the Brazilian TEF libraries all landed in the second
    group, each with its confidence stated rather than implied.
  * The verdict can now be PROVEN instead of guessed. A driver that Wine loads
    lands in an ordinary user process where the hardware calls are stubs: it
    starts, reads zeros, and the program misbehaves in a way that looks like
    its own bug. Wine prints that and only that. Tandem now reads it, and the
    two debug channels carrying it were re-enabled by name, because the log
    setting that keeps the log readable was switching off the evidence.
  * The machine's identity is held still. Under Wine most of what licensing
    software fingerprints is already the real machine, so the failure people
    actually report is not being refused - it is losing an activation when the
    environment is rebuilt, because the volume serial and MachineGuid are
    invented at random per prefix. Tandem now derives both from this machine
    and writes them at creation, so a rebuilt environment comes back as the
    same computer. It does not forge: no invented Windows ProductId, and the
    DMI spoof that would work is refused on the record in docs/IDEAS.md.
  * New command "tandem identidade": every identifier a program can read,
    side by side, each labelled - it is your real machine / it is a constant
    every Wine install reports / Tandem is holding it still. It reads /sys,
    /proc and system.reg and does not start Wine.
  * New command "tandem portas": where the pinpad, the scale, the printer and
    the serial scanner ended up. Wine reaches all of them already, and the two
    things that silently break it - a port numbered above COM4, which old
    point-of-sale software refuses, and missing dialout group membership - now
    have a message and a one-line fix each.

 -- ChrnX0 <rockx0@gmail.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0000

tandem (3.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The community list can now send, with permission. Until here it only ever
    pulled, which was the right default and had one measurable consequence:
    lista/lista.tsv is EMPTY. The mechanism worked and collected nothing.
  * The reason it collected nothing is not that people are unwilling. It is
    that contributing meant reading a line off a screen, copying it, opening a
    browser, creating an account on a site the owner has never heard of, and
    pasting it into an issue form. A shop owner does none of those five
    things, and an empty list is the measurement of how many did.
  * What makes automating it defensible is that the expensive half was already
    built and tested. t_lista_registro emits eight fields and t_lista_vaza
    REFUSES to emit them if a filename, a path, a username, a machine name or
    an IP address appears anywhere in the line. There is nothing to anonymise
    at send time because there was never anything identifying in it.
  * Off until the owner says otherwise, once, in his own language, looking at
    the actual line that would leave his machine - the eight fields printed,
    not a description of them. A permission dialog that describes a payload
    instead of displaying it is asking to be trusted rather than asking a
    question.
  * Asked in two places, and neither of them is the postinst: dpkg holds a
    lock while that runs, and a window waiting for a click there would hang
    apt until somebody noticed. So "tandem preparar" asks, which is the
    command the owner runs right after installing and where a dialog is
    already normal; and if he skips it, the question comes back once at the
    only moment it is concrete - just after he confirms that a program of his
    actually worked. There is also "tandem enviar" and an entry in the panel,
    because a decision he cannot find again is not a decision he made.
  * The sieve runs AGAIN at send time. Checking only when the record was built
    would trust that nothing touched the queue in between, and the queue is a
    plain-text file in the state directory - exactly the kind of thing that
    gets edited by hand.
  * Nothing blocks a double click. The record is queued, the send happens
    detached in the background, a machine with no internet keeps the line and
    forgets about it, and at most twenty lines leave per day so a machine
    cannot be turned into a firehose whatever it installs.
  * With nobody to ask - no window and no terminal - nothing is sent AND NO
    DECISION IS RECORDED. Writing "no" there would answer on the owner's
    behalf and never ask him again.
  * This build ships with no address to send to, on purpose, and says so out
    loud in "tandem enviar" instead of hiding it in a comment. An address
    means somebody hosts it, moderates it and answers for the data, which is a
    decision with a cost rather than a line of code. Everything else is built
    and tested against a real HTTP listener, so the day there is an address it
    is one assignment - and the queue means the lines learned before that day
    are not lost.
  * Two shortcuts that need no server at all, for whoever prefers to send by
    hand: "tandem contribuir" now copies the line to the clipboard and offers
    to open the issue form already filled in. Five steps become one.
  * A defect in the counter the owner is shown: "grep -c" on an EMPTY file
    prints 0 and then exits 1, so the fallback fired as well and "tandem
    enviar" reported the queue length as "0" followed by "0".
  * And one in the URL escaper, which dropped the last character of everything
    it encoded: the final line of od's output has no trailing newline, so
    "while read" returns non-zero on it and the loop body never runs for the
    last byte.
  * .apkm, declared since 3.0 and never exercised, is now covered - see 3.8.
  * The AppImage integration no longer executes the file in order to read it -
    the one place the project was still breaking its own rule. Every other
    reader here answers by reading; this one ran the downloaded binary to find
    out its name, so if the owner's answer had been "do not run this", it had
    already run. unsquashfs reads the payload at the offset already computed
    from the ELF header. Verified by reading the author's name out of an
    AppImage whose execute bit had been removed, which is proof it was not
    run. Falls back to the runtime when squashfs-tools is absent, and the log
    says which route it took, because that claim has to be checkable. Imported
    from Gear Lever, which refuses to execute the payload for the same reason.
  * And the name it reads is used in every message about the file, so a
    failure says "Inkscape nao abriu" instead of "Inkscape-1.2-x86_64.AppImage
    nao abriu" - a sentence about a program rather than about a filename.
    Imported from appimaged, which falls back to the basename exactly this
    way.
  * 26 new tests, 499 total, including the record going over a real socket and
    arriving byte for byte.

 -- Paulo Eduardo <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 07:00:00 +0000

tandem (3.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The five remaining install formats a person can double-click now open:
    .deb, .rpm, .flatpakref/.flatpakrepo, .snap, and shell installers. With
    the four from before and the two from 3.7 that is nine, which was the
    point - "one handler for every install format a person can double-click"
    is what docs/IDEAS.md argued the next real step was, and this is it.
  * Measured first, on the machine this runs on: NOBODY owns .deb, .rpm,
    .flatpakref or .snap there. The double click does nothing at all, exactly
    like an AppImage. That is not a bad message being improved, it is a vacuum
    being filled.
  * The whole .deb diagnosis happens BEFORE the password is asked, and that is
    a measured fact rather than a preference: "apt-get install -s" runs
    unprivileged and returns apt's own authoritative verdict with the unmet
    dependency names in it. There is no reason to make somebody type a
    password to be told no.
  * The message that case produces today is unusable, and it was copied off a
    real terminal rather than imagined: "programa-antigo : Depends: libssl1.1
    but it is not installable" followed by "E: Unable to correct problems, you
    have held broken packages." The owner held nothing. He downloaded the file
    the website offered him, and the real answer - built for a different
    version of your system, go back and pick the other one - is in neither
    line.
  * Two verdicts apt writes identically and that mean opposite things to the
    owner. A dependency with a release welded into its name (libssl1.1,
    libicu70, libwebkit2gtk-4.0-37) will never install here and there is
    nothing to try; a plain program name means a repository the machine has
    not been told about, and the website he downloaded from has the
    instructions. Telling him the wrong one costs him an afternoon.
  * debinfo.py reads a .deb by walking the ar archive and the control tarball
    by hand. build.py already writes that format without dpkg-deb, so reading
    it back is symmetric. Checked field by field against dpkg-deb on three
    real packages, including two current Ubuntu ones.
  * Every current Ubuntu .deb uses control.tar.zst, which Python cannot read
    on its own - and neither the zstd command nor python3-zstandard is
    installed on a normal machine. A reader without it would handle almost no
    real package. It goes through libzstd by ctypes instead: dpkg PRE-DEPENDS
    on libzstd1, so on any machine where a .deb means anything the library is
    there by construction. With a ceiling on the declared size, because a
    decompressor told to allocate a hostile number takes the machine down
    instead of reporting a bad file.
  * A .deb cut short is reported as an interrupted download. Both shapes: one
    where a member claims more bytes than the file has, and one cut exactly on
    a member boundary, where every header present is consistent and nothing
    looks wrong. The second was found by a test that passed for the wrong
    reason - the reader now also refuses a package carrying no data member.
  * A .deb that wants to REMOVE other programs asks first, with the list. That
    is the one way this path can do real damage, and apt announces it in a
    line the owner would scroll past.
  * Downgrades ask too, and which version is newer is dpkg's call, not a
    string comparison: Debian version ordering has epochs and tildes, and
    shell gets it wrong.
  * rpminfo.py reads the name, version, architecture and distribution out of
    an .rpm header without rpm installed - the lead, then a signature header
    padded to an eight-byte boundary, then the real one. With the name in hand
    the answer stops being a refusal: the same program is looked for in the
    machine's own repositories, and when it is there the owner gets "sudo apt
    install hello" instead of "no". Verified against a real Fedora package.
  * Converting an .rpm with alien is deliberately NOT offered, and the reason
    is written down: a converted package carries dependency names that do not
    exist here and skips the scripts that set the program up, producing
    something that looks installed and is not. That is the exact failure this
    project exists to prevent, so it is not offered even as a last resort.
  * A .flatpakref installs for the user without a password; a .flatpakrepo
    adds a SOURCE and installs nothing, and is said to be that in those words
    - treating the two the same would promise an application and hand over a
    repository.
  * A loose .snap can only be installed with a flag literally named
    --dangerous, and the owner is told so in his own language before it is
    used. A tool that passes --dangerous quietly is making that decision for
    him.
  * Shell installers are the one handler whose default answer is NOT to run
    the file. A script does anything its author wants; a makeself blob is
    meant to be run and gets that offer, while a small plain script gets
    offered as text first, because that is what somebody almost certainly
    wants with a file he just downloaded. application/x-shellscript is the one
    type tandem repair does NOT claim: opening a downloaded script in a text
    editor is a defensible default, and Tandem does not take a type from a
    handler already doing the right thing.
  * A silent failure, found and fixed before it shipped. A .flatpakrepo with
    no graphical session and no terminal exited 0 with ZERO BYTES of output.
    The cause is a distinction t_pergunta cannot make: "the owner clicked
    Cancel" may be silent, "there was nobody to ask" may not. Three refusal
    paths in the new handlers had it wrong. There is now a test that runs
    every handler against every fixture with no window and no terminal and
    demands a sentence - it is what found this, and nothing else would have.
  * apt and dpkg failures are translated from messages produced on purpose on
    a real Ubuntu and pasted in, not written from documentation. The dpkg lock
    is the one that matters most for a lay user: "Could not get lock
    /var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend. It is held by process 16003" becomes "the
    computer is already installing something else, wait a minute". It is
    ordered first because it is the cause and the broken-packages line is the
    consequence.
  * tandem doctor reports the package architecture, the extra architectures
    dpkg was told about, flatpak and snap; the self-test exercises the .deb
    reader on a real package and checks that apt answers without a password,
    because the whole diagnosis rests on that.
  * .apkm support existed on paper since 3.0 - a registered MIME type and a
    name in the reader's own table, with no fixture behind it, which is a
    promise rather than a feature. It is exercised now, and exercising it found
    something: some .apkm files are ENCRYPTED by the site that distributes
    them, and only that site's own installer opens one. The reader used to walk
    into that and come back with a Python exception in English about a missing
    password. It now recognises the encryption from the zip flag before reading
    anything, and the owner is told the file is locked by the site rather than
    broken.
  * 100 new tests, 473 total.

 -- Paulo Eduardo <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 05:00:00 +0000

tandem (3.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Two formats Linux uses itself now open with a double click: .AppImage and
    .jar. The reason is the same one that started this project - the owner
    downloaded something, clicked it, and nothing happened - and for these two
    the cause is not Wine, it is Linux.
  * An AppImage arrives from the browser without the execute bit, and a file
    without that bit is not offered to the system as a program at all. The
    click opens an archive manager, or does nothing, and the file gets blamed.
    Tandem sets the bit: the owner double-clicked, he has already said he wants
    to run it, and asking permission for the one thing that makes that possible
    would be theatre.
  * appimageinfo.py reads the header without executing anything. The AppImage
    generation and the processor it was built for live in the first twenty
    bytes, and the payload's own header says how many bytes it should have - so
    an interrupted download, which is the most common way an AppImage fails for
    a lay user, is diagnosed as an interrupted download instead of as a broken
    program. The payload offset it computes was checked against the runtime's
    own --appimage-offset on a real AppImage: same number, with nothing run.
  * FUSE is worked around instead of demanded. Ubuntu dropped libfuse2 from the
    default install in 22.04 and every second-generation AppImage mounts itself
    with it, so a fresh download on a current Zorin fails with a dlopen message
    and nothing else. Tandem recognises that failure and retries with
    --appimage-extract-and-run, which needs nothing installed; it says so
    afterwards, and only after the program actually worked. Measured with
    /dev/fuse removed: exit code 127, and the workaround opened the program.
  * The AppImage goes into the applications menu, using the desktop entry the
    AppImage itself carries. This is the same problem "tandem programas" exists
    for - on Wayland GNOME does not re-read the application list - except here
    it has a real fix. Extraction does not mount anything, so it works on
    exactly the machines that have no FUSE; that was verified, not assumed.
    An entry whose file has since been deleted removes itself.
  * jarinfo.py answers the two questions Java answers in words nobody outside
    the trade can act on. "no main manifest attribute" becomes "this file is a
    piece of a program, not a program" - the two kinds of .jar are
    indistinguishable from the outside, and the owner has no way of knowing he
    downloaded the wrong one. "UnsupportedClassVersionError: class file version
    65.0" becomes "it needs Java 21, you have 17": the number on the download
    page and the number in the error differ by 44.
  * Classes under META-INF/versions/ are excluded from that maximum on purpose.
    They are the multi-release mechanism, present so a NEWER Java can pick them
    up, and counting them demands a Java the program does not require. Checked
    against a real JVM: a jar carrying a class marked for a Java that does not
    exist yet still runs, and Tandem still says 21.
  * A manifest Class-Path is checked against the folder before running. Java's
    answer when a sibling jar is missing names a class, never the absent file.
    The 72-byte line folding is undone first - a wrapped Class-Path splits file
    names in the middle, and reading it line by line yields paths that do not
    exist.
  * A machine upgraded from an older version never claimed the new formats. The
    first-run mark was an empty file, so "already run once" meant "never again",
    and the double click on a .AppImage or a .jar went on doing nothing - the
    exact bug this project exists to kill, reintroduced by the mechanism
    meant to respect the owner's choices. Reproduced by installing 3.7 over
    3.6 on a real machine: .jar still answered openjdk-21-java.desktop, and the
    self-test said so out loud.
    The mark now records which version did the work. On an upgrade the prefix
    scan is NOT repeated, and tandem-repair runs in a new --somente-novos mode
    that claims only the types this machine has never claimed - kept in
    tipos-aplicados.txt. A type the owner has never seen Tandem own carries no
    decision of his to overwrite; one he re-pointed on purpose is left alone.
  * The type-to-handler lookup in tandem-repair matched a newline-separated list
    with a pattern looking for spaces, so every type came out associated with an
    empty handler - "application/java-archive=" - which reads to the system
    as no default at all and to a person as a job done. The suite caught it
    before it was ever committed; reading the code did not. An unresolvable
    handler is now refused and logged instead of written.
  * tandem preparar installs the Java and the FUSE library too, and doctor
    reports both, along with the machine's processor and how many AppImages
    Tandem has put in the menu. The self-test exercises the two new readers
    rather than listing them, and now checks the file association of all four
    formats instead of only the .exe.
  * tandem programas lists AppImages next to the Windows programs. For the
    owner they are the same thing - something he installed with Tandem.
  * A version number read off a file becomes part of a command that runs as
    root when Tandem offers to install the Java a program asks for. It is
    reduced to digits before it gets there.
  * tandem-apk reached for /usr/lib/tandem/apkinfo.py with the path nailed
    down, so exercising it from a working copy silently read the INSTALLED
    library instead. The executables all honour TANDEM_LIB; now the sibling
    binaries honour TANDEM_BIN for the same reason, and the panel can be
    exercised without installing the package.
  * The manual pointed at docs/FORMATO-LISTA.md, renamed when the repository
    went English.
  * A CI harness runs real Windows software through Tandem weekly - PuTTY,
    Notepad++, 7-Zip and WinMerge, pinned by sha256 - and looks at the screen
    afterwards with xdotool instead of trusting an exit code. It also
    cross-checks peinfo.py against objdump on every binary it downloads. The
    project's largest uncertainty was never the mechanism, it was the sample.

 -- Paulo Eduardo <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 03:00:00 +0000

tandem (3.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The whole repository is now written in English - comments, documentation,
    this changelog - and CLAUDE.md records that as a standing directive. Four
    deliberate exceptions, all for the same reason: the product's user is a
    Brazilian shop owner who is not a programmer. Strings shown to the user,
    the command names they type, anything shipped inside the package
    (man/tandem.1, the .desktop entries, the pt_BR MIME comments) and the
    LEIAME/CONTRIBUINDO pair stay in Portuguese. The short form: the
    repository is English, the product is Portuguese.
  * Five defects found by an adversarial audit, each one reproduced on the
    machine before being believed.
  * A multi-line value corrupted the memory file permanently. The format is
    one KEY=VALUE per line and the bitness message is five lines; the rewrite
    filter only removed the first physical line, so every run left the
    continuation lines behind as orphans and appended a fresh copy. Four runs,
    twenty-two lines, three blocks of garbage, growing forever. Values are now
    escaped on write and unescaped on read, and the rewrite drops orphan lines
    left by earlier versions.
  * The rescue copy told the truth about only two of its three outcomes.
    "There was nothing to save" and "there was data and the copy FAILED" both
    returned 1, and all three destructive paths carried on deleting either
    way - reproduced with a full disk. They are now distinct codes, and a
    failed copy stops the deletion: the double-click path refuses outright,
    the two interactive ones ask with the risk spelled out.
  * The data search lost the exit status of its own find by running it inside
    a pipe. A prefix big enough to hit the 60 s timeout looked exactly like a
    prefix with no data at all - to a caller that was about to delete
    something. The timeout now propagates as a failed copy.
  * wmvcore.dll pointed at wmp9 for every program. wmp9 has no real win64
    branch: its payload is the 32-bit XP installer, which lands in syswow64
    and is useless to a 64-bit program. wmp11 does have one, and its overrides
    are a strict superset. A 64-bit program asking for wmvcore.dll now gets
    wmp11.
  * The list of 32-bit-only verbs is now classified from the INSTALLED
    winetricks instead of being written down here. The fixed list named eight
    verbs; an inventory found 42, 38 of them reachable from a real Wine log. A
    list that only covers what somebody remembered to add covers nothing, and
    winetricks is updated from outside this project. The eight remain as the
    fallback for when winetricks cannot be read.
  * The README and LEIAME stopped pointing the main installation path at an
    empty Releases page. Until a release exists, they show the build, which
    needs neither a Debian host nor dpkg-deb.
  * The test suite is in English too, and the runner API with it: pass, fail,
    equal, skip, section. A blanket rename is exactly how a test file gets
    damaged invisibly - rewrite an EXPECTED value or a "case" pattern along
    with the prose around it and the test keeps passing while comparing the
    wrong thing. It happened during this very change: *...continua faltando*
    became *...continua missing*, and the pattern stopped matching the message
    Tandem actually produces. Both lists are now guarded by a checksum, so the
    next bulk edit cannot touch them quietly.
  * 309 tests.

 -- Paulo Eduardo <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 09 Aug 2026 02:00:00 +0000

tandem (3.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The repository is now ready for people from outside: CONTRIBUTING.md and
    CONTRIBUINDO.md carry the five rules that do not bend and the evidence
    bar; the issue templates already ask for the output of "tandem socorro"
    and the line from "tandem contribuir"; there is a PR template; and a
    release workflow builds the .deb, runs the suite and lintian, really
    installs and purges it, and only then publishes. The README had been
    pointing at Releases for thirteen versions and no release existed.
  * Real screenshots of the program's own windows in the README and LEIAME,
    and both now list all 20 commands. They described 9, and claimed Tandem
    does not install Wine or Waydroid, which "tandem preparar" has been doing
    since 2.x.
  * Everything written in the repository is now in English - comments, test
    names, documentation, this changelog. Messages to the user stay in
    Portuguese: that is rule number 2, and the target user is a Brazilian shop
    owner who is not a programmer.
  * The verb is now chosen by the program's bitness. A verb-by-verb survey of
    the installed winetricks: most modern verbs already install both payloads
    on their own, exactly ONE has a 64-bit sibling (xact -> xact_x64), and
    eight are 32-bit-only with no sibling at all. For those eight, on a 64-bit
    program, Tandem warns BEFORE installing that it probably will not help -
    instead of the owner finding out after half an hour of downloading. It
    does not block: it explains and lets them decide.
  * Delivery proof now also applies on the memory/list shortcut, which was the
    one path still writing the receipt from the winetricks exit code alone -
    bypassing the entire 3.3 thesis, and doing so exactly where the lesson
    comes from outside and deserves more suspicion, not less. The same
    shortcut gained the suspend inhibitor (it can also run dotnet48, at half
    an hour) and the verb-name validation that recipes already had and the
    list did not.
  * The list's machine count reaches the user: "on 340 machines this program
    needed these components" is the one thing the list knows that no forum
    does. It was specified, read by a function, tested, and never shown.
  * Fetching the list distinguishes "no internet" from "the list does not
    exist yet". A 404 was sending the owner to check their router because of a
    file that had not been published.
  * The documentation-drift test now EXTRACTS the commands from the main case
    statement instead of using a fixed list. With a fixed list it went green
    while four new commands were in no document at all.
  * Recipes were teaching the wrong import syntax, so whoever received one hit
    a usage error. The manual gained "version", "help", the five directories
    Tandem creates and the environment variables; and its date, frozen at
    2025-01-01 for thirteen versions, became real.
  * "curl | wget" enters Recommends: the community list depends on one of the
    two and neither was declared.
  * 294 tests.

